### 8. Quality Assurance Plan

Quality assurance will be performed throughout the development lifecycle rather than only before deployment. Developers and testers will follow coding standards, review changes, maintain test cases, and verify every completed feature.

All reported defects should include:

* Defect title
* Description of the problem
* Steps to reproduce
* Expected result
* Actual result
* Severity and priority
* Screenshot or error log
* Assigned developer
* Current status

Defects may be categorized as Critical, High, Medium, or Low. After a developer fixes a defect, the tester will retest it and perform regression testing on related features.

### 9. Feedback and Continuous Improvement

Feedback will be collected from students, faculty members, administrators, testers, and project reviewers. The feedback will be reviewed regularly to identify usability problems, missing requirements, and possible improvements.

The team will use short testing cycles, code reviews, issue tracking, and regular progress meetings to improve product quality. Lessons learned from each testing cycle will be documented and applied to future development activities.

### 10. Entry and Exit Criteria

Testing can begin when the required feature is implemented, the test environment is available, and test data has been prepared.

Testing can be considered complete when:

* All planned test cases have been executed
* Critical and high-severity defects are resolved
* Major features work as expected
* Regression testing is completed
* User acceptance feedback is reviewed
* The final testing report is prepared

### 11. Conclusion

This testing strategy provides a structured approach for validating the SmartCampus Helpdesk application. By combining unit, integration, system, acceptance, manual, and automated testing, the project team can identify defects early and deliver a reliable, secure, and user-friendly system. Continuous feedback, proper bug tracking, and regression testing will support continuous improvement and help ensure that the application is ready for deployment.
